#use(std/code)

#section{Control flow}::

A YAMD document is a Racket program, so control flow needs no special syntax:
it is ordinary host code inside a #code{#(...)} escape. On its own line the
escape is a statement; inline it is an expression whose value becomes content.
A loop in a body position splices the content it returns.

#code|{#(for/list ([n (in-range 1 16)]) …)}| runs its body fifteen times and
splices the fifteen values it returns. Each #code{#item} re-enters markup, and
#code{#(n)} reads the loop variable — an inline #code{#(if …)} tags the parity:

#list(style: "1")
	#(for/list ([n (in-range 1 16)])
		#item{Item #(n) — #(if (even? n) "even" "odd")})

Statement-position #code{#(if …)} chooses whole blocks of content:

#(define ready #t)

#(if ready
	#para{The system is #strong{ready}.}
	#para{Still warming up…})

For several branches #code{#(cond …)} is clearest. A #code{#(define …)}
statement names a helper the loop then calls — here the classic fizz/buzz over
the same fifteen numbers:

#(define (label n)
	(cond
		[(zero? (modulo n 15)) "fizzbuzz"]
		[(zero? (modulo n 3)) "fizz"]
		[(zero? (modulo n 5)) "buzz"]
		[else (number->string n)]))

#list
	#(for/list ([n (in-range 1 16)])
		#item{#(label n)})
